# B4 — Feature Success Evaluation

⸻

1️⃣ Problem Clarification

Leadership asked to “use ML to determine whether the feature was successful.”

That framing is incorrect.

The real question is:

Did the feature cause a meaningful change in business outcomes?

This is a causal impact problem, not a prediction problem.

Decision-Makers
	•	Product leadership
	•	Growth / Monetization teams
	•	Executive stakeholders

Decision Informed
	•	Keep or roll back the feature
	•	Expand rollout
	•	Invest further or iterate
	•	Sunset

Plain-English Problem Statement

Determine whether the newly launched feature caused a statistically and practically meaningful change in key business outcomes, while controlling for trends, seasonality, and confounders.

⸻

2️⃣ Problem Type Classification

Primary framing:
	•	Causal analysis / experimentation

If available:
	•	A/B testing

If not randomized:
	•	Difference-in-Differences (DiD)
	•	Interrupted Time Series (ITS)
	•	Synthetic Control

Not appropriate:
	•	Supervised prediction models as “proof of success”
	•	Correlation-based regression without causal structure

Prediction answers what will happen.
This question asks what changed because of the feature.

⸻

3️⃣ Data Science Scope

Likely Available Data
	•	User-level engagement metrics
	•	Conversion rates
	•	Retention (D1, D7, D30)
	•	Revenue metrics
	•	Feature exposure logs
	•	Feature usage logs
	•	Rollout timestamps
	•	Cohort identifiers
	•	Device / region segmentation
	•	Marketing calendar

Granularity
	•	User-level
	•	Session-level
	•	Time-series (pre vs post)
	•	Cohort-level (treated vs control)

Key Data Risks
	•	Seasonality effects
	•	Concurrent marketing campaigns
	•	Overlapping feature releases
	•	Selection bias (early adopters)
	•	Survivorship bias
	•	Simpson’s paradox in aggregated metrics

⸻

4️⃣ Metric Hierarchy

Success cannot be evaluated with a single raw metric.

North Star Metric

The primary outcome aligned with feature intent, e.g.:
	•	Conversion rate
	•	Paid activation
	•	Weekly retained users
	•	Revenue per active user

Only one primary success metric should determine the decision.

⸻

Drivers (Explain the Why)
	•	Feature adoption rate
	•	Repeat usage
	•	Funnel completion
	•	Time-to-first-value
	•	Engagement depth

Drivers explain how impact happened.

⸻

Guardrails (Do No Harm)
	•	Crash rate
	•	Latency
	•	Refunds
	•	Support tickets
	•	Churn
	•	Negative feedback

A feature can increase the North Star while damaging core product health. Guardrails prevent false wins.

⸻

5️⃣ Concrete Evaluation Plan
	1.	Lock the metric hierarchy before analysis
Prevents metric shopping.
	2.	Define treatment exposure precisely
Who had the feature? When? Did they actually use it?
	3.	Select the evaluation design
	•	A/B test if randomized
	•	DiD / ITS if staggered rollout
	•	Synthetic control if no clean control
	4.	Validate pre-trends and comparability
Ensure treated and control groups behaved similarly before launch.
	5.	Estimate impact with uncertainty
Report:
	•	Lift %
	•	Confidence intervals or Bayesian posterior
	•	Effect size (practical significance)
	6.	Segment cautiously for heterogeneity
Only pre-defined segments (e.g., region, device, new vs existing users).
	7.	Review guardrails
Confirm no hidden harm.
	8.	Produce a decision memo
Clear recommendation:
	•	Expand
	•	Iterate
	•	Re-test
	•	Roll back

⸻

6️⃣ Use of LLMs

LLMs were used to:
	•	Explore causal frameworks beyond predictive ML
	•	Enumerate quasi-experimental options
	•	Stress-test whether supervised learning was appropriate

LLM tendency to default to predictive modeling was explicitly rejected.
Causal attribution was kept central.

⸻

7️⃣ Unknown Methods Discovery

Synthetic Control

When no randomized control exists:
	•	Construct a weighted “virtual control” group
	•	Match pre-launch trends
	•	Measure divergence post-launch

Initial incorrect assumption:

No A/B test means no credible evaluation.

Correction:

Quasi-experimental methods can provide structured causal inference when properly designed.

⸻

Why This Case Matters

This case demonstrates:
	•	Disciplined problem framing
	•	Clear separation between prediction and causation
	•	Structured metric hierarchy
	•	Decision-grade evaluation
	•	Appropriate use of ML only where it adds value
